## Observation 18: Post-Respiratory Exercise Noon Wake-Up Notes

Time: 02:20 HST (Approximate)
Date: 2026-09-20
Location: Honolulu, Oahu, Hawaii

### Context

This observation occurred after waking and following the previous respiratory-awareness session involving:

- Throat-focused nasal breathing
- Expansion and contraction exercises
- Airway mapping
- Chest, back, and torso awareness exercises

At the time of this observation, no attempt had yet been made to inhale or exhale through the nose.

### Observation

### Current Breathing Method

- Breathing continued exclusively through the mouth.
- No nasal inhalation or exhalation was intentionally performed.
- Sensation remained present within the facial skin and facial tissues.

### Findings

- Facial sensation remained accessible upon waking.
- Awareness of facial structures appeared more available than during previous periods of reduced awareness.
- Breathing through the mouth continued without immediate use of the nasal passages.

---

### Observation: Internal Mouth Breathing Awareness

### Description

While continuing to breathe through the mouth, attention was directed toward different internal areas involved in breathing.

### Findings

- Normal mouth breathing appears to rely primarily on commonly used pathways inside the mouth.
- Breathing using these familiar pathways feels associated with airflow moving directly toward the chest and lungs.
- Increased awareness of deeper regions of the mouth and head is more difficult to maintain during normal mouth breathing.
- The sensation associated with breathing tends to follow a predictable pathway into the chest.

### Learning

The commonly used breathing pathway appears easier to access than deeper internal regions currently being explored through attention and awareness exercises.

---

### Observation: Difficulty Redirecting Awareness Toward the Head

### Description

Attempts were made to increase awareness and sensation toward areas associated with the head and brain.

### Findings

- Awareness consistently shifted toward the face when attention was directed upward.
- Sensations appeared easier to maintain in facial regions than in deeper areas of the head.
- Upward-directed attention did not produce the same level of awareness beyond the facial region.

### Learning

Facial regions currently appear more accessible to awareness than deeper regions of the head.

---

### Observation: Gravity-Assisted Head Awareness

### Description

A position change was introduced by leaning forward with the head directed downward while continuing mouth breathing.

### Findings

- Increased stimulation and awareness were noticed within head-related regions.
- Head-down positioning appeared associated with stronger sensations in areas above the neck.
- The effect occurred while continuing exclusive mouth breathing.

### Learning

Body position may influence subjective awareness and sensation distribution during breathing exercises.

---

### Research Reflection

A working question emerged during this observation:

> Could habitual breathing patterns contribute to differences in awareness between various regions of the face, mouth, throat, and head?

At present, this remains a research question and not a conclusion.

### Additional Notes

- The objective remains to reproduce observed awareness patterns without relying on environmental assistance or external variables.
- Future observations should distinguish between:
  - Changes resulting from attention.
  - Changes resulting from posture.
  - Changes resulting from breathing method.
  - Changes resulting from other external factors.

### Confidence

Low to Medium

### Verification Required

Yes

